		<dc:title id="title">Some Experiences of an Irish R.M.</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#title">Some Experiences of an Irish R.M.</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">Humorous stories, English</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">sh85062977</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-2">Ireland--Social life and customs--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-2">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-2">sh2008115515</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-3">British--Ireland--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-3">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-3">sh2007102051</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Comedy</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Shorts</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">An army major takes up a post as a magistrate in the West of Ireland, where his landlord, his servants, and the local people outwit him at every turn.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;Major Sinclair Yeates obtains a post as Resident Magistrate in the West of Ireland. He rents a half-repaired country house from Flurry Knox, a young horse dealer and master of the local hounds who, as Yeates puts it, looks “like a stable boy among gentlemen, and a gentleman among stable boys.” Yeates expects to uphold the law. Instead, Flurry and his many relations draw him into horse deals, hunts, race meetings, and dances, and Yeates is usually the last to understand what is happening.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/e-oe-somerville"&gt;E. Œ. Somerville&lt;/a&gt; and her cousin &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/martin-ross"&gt;Martin Ross&lt;/a&gt; wrote the twelve stories for the &lt;i&gt;Badminton Magazine&lt;/i&gt;, and published them as a book in 1899 with illustrations by Somerville. Two further collections followed, in 1908 and 1915.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
